00:18:47.949 --> 00:18:49.477
I'm reading the Quran as you see.

00:18:49.807 --> 00:18:51.193
Dr. Khalifa: I'm only reading the Quran.

00:18:51.193 --> 00:18:58.385
A man: (unintelligible)

00:19:00.385 --> 00:19:01.913
(17:46) وَإِذَا ذَكَرْتَ رَبَّكَ

00:19:01.913 --> 00:19:04.763
I'm trying to find out if I am an idol worshipper or not.

00:19:05.093 --> 00:19:06.243
And here is the criterion.

00:19:06.243 --> 00:19:10.839
(17:46) إِذَا ذَكَرْتَ رَبَّكَ فِى ٱلْقُرْءَانِ وَحْدَهُۥ وَلَّوْا۟ عَلَىٰٓ أَدْبَـٰرِهِمْ نُفُورًۭا

00:19:10.839 --> 00:19:14.538
And when you preach your Lord using the Quran alone,

00:19:14.538 --> 00:19:17.246
(17:46) ٱلْقُرْءَانِ وَحْدَهُۥ وَلَّوْا۟ عَلَىٰٓ أَدْبَـٰرِهِمْ نُفُورًۭا

00:19:17.246 --> 00:19:18.821
They turn away in aversion.

00:19:18.821 --> 00:19:22.284
Some people believe that the word Wadahu (وَحْدَهُ)
refers to Rabbaka (رَبَّكَ)

00:19:22.284 --> 00:19:27.305
(17:46) وَإِذَا ذَكَرْتَ رَبَّكَ فِى ٱلْقُرْءَانِ وَحْدَهُۥ وَلَّوْا۟ عَلَىٰٓ أَدْبَـٰرِهِمْ نُفُورًۭا

00:19:27.966 --> 00:19:35.205
If you preach your Lord alone, in the Quran alone, without anything else besides the Quran, they turn away in aversion. They don't like it.

00:19:35.205 --> 00:19:35.978
This is my story.

00:19:35.978 --> 00:19:39.749
If I, I don't like somebody preaching God
to me using the Quran alone,

00:19:39.749 --> 00:19:44.019
this would tell me that I'm an idol worshipper and that I
don't know that I am an idol worshipper.

00:19:44.019 --> 00:19:46.514
I am unaware of being an idol worshipper.

00:19:46.514 --> 00:19:49.104
This is what Allah Subhanahu Wa Ta'ala teaches
us in Quran.

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:55.950
Now, some people try to tell me that I don't know
the grammar of the Quran because

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:58.516
وَإِذَا ذَكَرْتَ رَبَّكَ فِى ٱلْقُرْءَانِ

00:19:58.516 --> 00:19:59.478
which is mansūb (منصوب)

00:19:59.478 --> 00:20:01.289
Wahdahu (وَحْدَهُ) which is mansūb (منصوب)

00:20:01.289 --> 00:20:03.478
This means Wahdahu (وَحْدَهُ) refers to the Zakar (ذَكَرْ).

